    {
      "id": "9e6a8e",
      "name": "fr-notes",
      "parent": "275f01",
      "children": [],
      "settings": {
        "notesContent": "<p>Note: If you don't have at least 18 unique images, this frame probably isn't a good choice.</p><p><strong>How it works:</strong></p><p>This frame uses pure CSS animation and no JS. For this reason, it uses image duplication to mimic infinite scrolling with the image duplicates set to aria hidden so they aren't duplicated for screen readers.</p><p>We recommend you use query loops to populate the sliders. Place the query loop on the \"Slider Image Wrapper\" element. You'll see two of these in each Slider by default. The second one is the \"hidden\" one and its loop should be an exact copy of the first loop.</p><p>For the loops in the subsequent sliders, configure them the same way, but use an offset equal to the number of images in the previous loop(s) using the offset field. This will ensure that the sliders don't repeat each others' images.</p><p>If you want to use manual images instead of query loops, make sure you're duplicating the \"Slider Image Wrapper\" element and make sure you have your duplicates setup properly with aria hidden on the duplicates.</p><p><strong>How do I adjust the speed?</strong></p><p>You can change the value of the <strong>--slide-speed</strong> variable in Sliders (CSS).</p><p><strong>How do i adjust the overlay?</strong></p><p>You can change the value of the overlay variables in Sliders (CSS).</p><p><strong>How do I adjust the height of the sliders?</strong></p><p>There's a max-height token defined on Sliders (CSS).</p>",
        "_cssGlobalClasses": [
          "ieofwl"
        ],
        "_margin": {
          "top": "var(--space-xl)",
          "bottom": "var(--space-xl)"
        }
      }
    }
